<section>
<title>Problems</title>
<exercise xml:id="prob_Schwarzschild_dimensions">
<statement>
<p>
Use dimensional analysis on the definition of the Schwarzschild radius to
show that it has dimensions of length.
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="prob_Schwarzschild_radius">
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the Schwarzschild radius for the following objects.
<ol>
<li><p>A neutron star with mass <m>6.0\times 10^{30}\,\text{kg}</m>.</p></li>
<li><p>Jupiter.</p></li>
</ol>

</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="prob_Schwarzschild_distance">
<statement>
<p>
You find yourself near a black hole with the coordinate <m>r</m> equal to
three times the Schwarzschild radius.
<ol>
<li><p>How much does the coordinate <m>r</m> increase if you move a distance
<m>10\,\text{m}</m> directly away from the black hole.</p></li>
<li><p>How would you measure this increase in <m>r</m>?</p></li>
</ol>

</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="prob_Schwarzschild_time">
<statement>
<p>
You still find yourself near a black hole with the coordinate <m>r</m> equal
to three times the Schwarzschild radius.
<ol>
<li><p>A time of <m>10\,\text{s}</m> elapses on your clock.  In that time
    interval how many seconds elapse on a clock very far away?</p></li>
<li><p>You move to a new location where for every <m>10\,\text{s}</m> that
  elapses on your clock, <m>20\,\text{s}</m> elapses on the remote clock.
  Determine your new coordinate <m>r</m> in terms of the Schwarzschild
  radius.</p></li>
</ol>

</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="prob_Hare_and_Tortoise">
<statement>
<p>
Special relativity review! The <em>Millennium Tortoise</em> and the
<em>Millennium Hare</em> are racing from Earth to Alpha Centauri, a
distance of <m>4\,\text{lt-yr}</m> in the Earth frame. The
<em>Tortoise</em> travels at a constant speed of <m>0.5c</m>. The
<em>Hare</em> waits on Earth for 3 years after the <em>Tortoise</em>
departs, and then moves at the blazing speed of <m>0.8c</m> to Alpha Centauri.
<ol>
<li><p>Show that the <em>Tortoise</em> and the <em>Hare</em> arrive at
   Alpha Centauri at the same time.</p></li>
<li><p>Calculate how much time has elapsed for the <em>Tortoise</em>
  during this trip.</p></li>
<li><p>Calculate how much time has elapsed for the <em>Hare</em> during
  this trip.</p></li>
</ol>

</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
In the movie <em>Interstellar</em> the crew visits a planet that is
orbiting a black hole. The gravitational time dilation is so
strong that one hour spent on the planet corresponds to 7 years of
time far away from the black hole. Calculate the coordinate <m>r</m> of
the planet's location in terms of the Schwarzschild radius. Express
your answer as <m>r=(1 + \text{ small number } )R_s</m>, that is, calculate
the small number.
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
A neutron star has mass of <m>6.0\times 10^{30}\,\text{kg}</m> and a
circumference of <m>2\pi\times 15\,\text{km}</m>. An object orbits this
star in a circular orbit with circumference <m>2\pi\times
  15.01\,\text{km}</m>. How far above the surface of the neutron star
is the object?
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="prob_trip">
<statement>
<p>
For path (b) in <xref ref="fig_trip2">Figure</xref>, assume you travel at velocity
<m>0.8c</m> for the first leg, and remain stationary, <m>v = 0</m>, for the rest
of the trip.
<ol>
<li><p>Calculate the <m>t</m>-coordinate at the junction point.</p></li>
<li><p>Determine the proper time for each leg and add them to get
      the total proper time.</p></li>
<li><p>Compare to the proper time of <m>0.8\, \mbox{s}</m> for the
      direct route.</p></li>
</ol>

</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
Explain why gravitational lensing could cause a distant point-source of
light, such as a distant galaxy, to look like a ring.
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="prob_black_hole">
<statement>
<p>
Consider a black hole with the mass of the Sun.
<ol>
<li><p>Determine the largest radius that this black hole could have.</p></li>
<li><p>Determine the minimum density that this black hole must have.</p></li>
</ol>

</p>
</statement>
</exercise>
</section>
